    callout: "Rated at the floor. Length of stay was unrelated to post-trip happiness in a 1,530-person study; travel expenditure showed an inverted-U with life satisfaction; tourists who exceeded their budget were less satisfied; overspending on shopping weakened the satisfaction chain; and vacation 'maximizers' who exhaustively optimized their trip reported lower satisfaction and well-being. Spend on relaxation, company and novelty — not on more days or more stars.",
    feeds: ["Financial sanity", "Trip satisfaction"],
    description:
      "The money-and-length myth is one of the best-refuted ideas in tourism research. What predicts trip well-being is the quality of recovery experiences — relaxation, detachment, mastery, control — and who you share it with; what predicts trip regret is overspending and over-optimizing. A short, relaxed, well-planned trip within budget beats a long, expensive one you spent months agonizing over.",
    action: "Set the trip budget before you look at options, choose from three alternatives at most, and decide in one sitting; then stop researching and start anticipating.",
